{"step_index":925,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-10T20:55:40Z","input_tokens":2214,"cache_read_tokens":356740,"output_tokens":2007,"content":"Right now in [index.html](file:///c:/Users/CxAdmin/Desktop/Exploit/index.html) and [teacher.html](file:///c:/Users/CxAdmin/Desktop/Exploit/teacher.html), the Firebase words are gone, **but the JavaScript code itself is still human-readable**:\n- An AI can still read `fetch(url)` and notice `_dec(_vB, 73)` decodes a single array using a simple 4-line LCG loop (`s = (s * 31 + 17) & 255`).\n- All grading logic, anti-cheat rules, and API request construction sit in readable JS.\n\nHere are the concrete ways to encrypt and harden the JavaScript code itself, from zero-dependency static layering up to full AST obfuscation:\n\n---\n\n### Layer 2A: Encrypted Self-Unpacking Runtime (Zero-Dependency Static HTML)\nKeep clean editable source templates (e.g., `src/index.html`, `src/teacher.html`) + a small local Node packager that outputs hardened `index.html` and `teacher.html`:\n1. **Separate Data Container in HTML DOM:** Move `LOCAL_ENCRYPTED_VAULT` out of JS variables into `<script id=\"local-db-vault\" type=\"application/octet-stream\"></script>` so `injectVault()` in [teacher.html](file:///c:/Users/CxAdmin/Desktop/Exploit/teacher.html) never depends on plaintext JS variable names.\n2. **Encrypt the Entire `<script>` Body:** Encrypt the full JS codebase (`EncryptedLocalDB`, `fetch()`, anti-cheat listeners, timer, scoring) with a **multi-round cipher (KSA+PRGA RC4 + rolling CBC XOR)** into an unreadable `<script id=\"local-runtime-cipher\" type=\"application/octet-stream\">` payload.\n3. **Obfuscated Bootstrapper with Split Key Derivation:** Only a small, mangled bootstrapper remains visible in `<script>`, assembling the key from non-linear math + DOM/page fragments (no single `_vK` array literal) and executing the decrypted code purely in memory (`new Function(...)`).\n   - **AI Effect:** Feeding `index.html` or `teacher.html` to an AI reveals no readable `fetch()`, no readable `_vB` URL array, and no readable grading logic — only encrypted local data blocks (`local-db-vault` + `local-runtime-cipher`).\n\n---\n\n### Layer 2B: Full AST Obfuscati\n<truncated 173 bytes>\nncy `index.html` & `teacher.html`):\n1. **Control Flow Flattening:** Breaks every `if/else`, loop, and function (`readQuiz`, `finish`, `refreshSubmissions`) into scrambled `switch-case` state machines inside `while` loops so neither humans nor LLMs can read linear execution flow.\n2. **RC4 String Array + Rotation + Split Strings:** Strips every remaining string literal (`\"quizzes\"`, `\"active\"`, HTTP methods, CSS selectors, text) into shuffled, RC4-encrypted lookup tables.\n3. **Identifier Mangling + Dead Code Injection:** Renames all symbols to hex identifiers (`_0x...`) and injects fake branches to exhaust LLM reasoning if someone attempts static deobfuscation.\n4. **Encrypted Runtime Shell:** Wraps the obfuscated output inside the multi-round ciphered block so casual inspection doesn't even see raw JS at all.\n   *(Note: We should avoid aggressive `debugProtection` infinite-debugger loops so mobile browsers and normal student tabs don't freeze.)*\n\n---\n\n### Honest Limits to Keep in Mind\n- **Static Code vs. Live Network Tab:** Stronger JS encryption makes the `.html` file practically unreadable to AI text analysis and `View Source`. However, as long as the browser talks directly to Google's REST endpoint without a server proxy, opening **DevTools → Network** while running the quiz will still show the outbound HTTP call to `firestore.googleapis.com` (and an advanced attacker dynamically hooking `window.fetch` at runtime can log any URL passed to it). Only adding a Vercel `/api/` proxy (Approach A) hides the network destination too.\n\n---\n\n### Next Step\nWhich level would you like me to design in `implementation_plan.md`?\n1. **Layer 2A** (Zero-dependency Node packager encr