mentById`. This method circumvents string replacement vulnerabilities introduced by the obfuscation. Replacing the vault content using this approach in `teacher.html` is robust to obfuscation. This leverages the element's ID as a reliable anchor, even after code transformation. Now to test across different environments.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"search_web","args":{"query":"\"javascript-obfuscator selfDefending controlFlowFlattening inline script html quirks breaks\""}},{"name":"search_web","args":{"query":"\"javascript-obfuscator stringArrayEncoding rc4 compact true no comments options\""}}]}
{"step_index":928,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-10T20:58:17Z","content":"Created At: 2026-11-11T04:58:17+08:00\nCompleted At: 2026-11-11T04:58:21+08:00\nCreated At: 2026-11-11T04:58:17+08:00\nCompleted At: 2026-11-11T04:58:21+08:00\nThe search for \"javascript-obfuscator selfDefending controlFlowFlattening inline script html quirks breaks\" returned the following summary:\nWhen using **`javascript-obfuscator`** (or tools via obfuscator.io) with features like **`selfDefending`** and **`controlFlowFlattening`** inside **inline HTML scripts**, scripts frequently break, throw errors (such as `Maximum call stack size exceeded`)[1], or enter infinite loops[2]. \n\nThe collision of these configurations creates distinct parsing and runtime issues:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Why `selfDefending` Breaks Inline Scripts\nThe `selfDefending` option works by taking a stringified representation of the script function (`Function.prototype.toString()`), checking its formatting, spacing, or structure, and crashing the runtime (often triggering an infinite recursion loop) if it detects that the code has been altered, beautified, or reformatted[2][1].\n\n**The HTML Inline Script Conflict:**\n* **Whitespace & Newlines:** HTML parsers, template engines (like Jinja, Blade, EJS), or HTML minifiers automatically strip, add, or alter whitespace, indentation, and line breaks inside `<script>` tags. \n* **Character Escaping:** Inline scripts embedded in HTML often deal with HTML entity decoding (e.g., `<`, `>`, `&amp;`). \n* **The Result:** Because the code string evaluated by `selfDefending` at runtime differs textually from the raw code generated by the obfuscator (due to browser HTML parser normalization or formatting changes), **`selfDefending` assumes the code has been tampered with and intentionally breaks execution[2][1].**\n\n### 2. Why `controlFlowFlattening` Causes Issues in Inline Contexts\n`controlFlowFlattening` bloats the script by wrapping blocks inside massive `switch` statements driven by state-machine arrays[3]. \n\n**The HTML Inline Script Conflict:**\n* **String Length Limits & Parsing Quirk:** Inline scripts are parsed directl\n<truncated 624 bytes>\ne inside an HTML file, turn `selfDefending` off. Reserve `selfDefending` exclusively for external JavaScript files loaded via `<script src=\"...\"></script>`.\n   ```json\n   {\n     \"selfDefending\": false,\n     \"controlFlowFlattening\": true\n   }\n   ```\n\n2. **Move Code to External Files:**\n   Instead of using inline scripts, place your protected logic into a separate `.js` file and load it normally:\n   ```html\n   <script src=\"/js/protected.min.js\"></script>\n   ```\n   This prevents the HTML parser from mutating whitespace or breaking string-signature checks required by `selfDefending`.\n\n3. **Tune Control Flow Flattening Intensity:**\n   If you must use `controlFlowFlattening`, lower its threshold so it doesn't destabilize the inline execution context:\n   ```json\n   {\n     \"controlFlowFlattening\": true,\n     \"controlFlowFlatteningThreshold\": 0.2\n   }\n   ```\n\n4. **Beware of HTML Minifiers:**\n   Ensure that build pipelines (like HTMLWebpackPlugin, html-minifier) are not running post-processing passes that compress or modify the inner text of your obfuscated script tags.\n\nSources:\n[1] [github.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQG-ICA_kLai4Dd2BEtSi1aPy0LvitsIbnjM5WhQlsHY9RSv0_pJXoG1xOTe48psFGEQQjgJwDakboo-mbsIITcwyD8hswXAgOKc4XaDzDL2G2dn88la-F27vJFj0dZ0HfAMOMAsI_f9tCn853NJ-SxYfdz4y2jldFdqIsmhjEkx)\n[2] [stackoverflow.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFR7Gs7rhKkOiPqDrNWg0oLUALlCFgCFKDZH16snNXV-sOxkXilv_ErgOCj0tpEctuCmqANkqPEhB7TCyKYVST7bhW6_iZTX2O4aznKwfZSc59UP1pnw02SEHMTVhlyRnJoAbDs8ChIBsxwBji1WLsAoNI45bUQsWmsZ1fu8JFqNN-8hD_3Rh0N8L3aR7k1iJZEjqPbOT00djCgOhhcgYslGOUAfP0jtuGayWac7_UuWxvTzA==)\n[3] [github.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/ground