{"step_index":929,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-10T20:58:17Z","content":"Created At: 2026-11-11T04:58:17+08:00\nCompleted At: 2026-11-11T04:58:25+08:00\nCreated At: 2026-11-11T04:58:17+08:00\nCompleted At: 2026-11-11T04:58:25+08:00\nThe search for \"javascript-obfuscator stringArrayEncoding rc4 compact true no comments options\" returned the following summary:\nHere is how you can use these options for **`javascript-obfuscator`** via the Command Line Interface (CLI) or as a Node.js JSON configuration object.\n\n### 1. Command Line Interface (CLI)\nTo run it directly from your terminal, format your command like this[1][2]:\n\n```bash\njavascript-obfuscator input.js --output output.js --compact true --string-array-encoding rc4 --unicode-escape-sequence false\n```\n*(Note: Explicitly passing a \"no comments\" flag isn't usually necessary because `javascript-obfuscator` strips out standard comments and formatting by default unless source maps or specific preservation options are enabled. Also, depending on your version of `javascript-obfuscator`, `stringArrayEncoding` can accept `rc4`[2] or an array format like `['rc4']`[3]).*\n\n---\n\n### 2. Node.js Configuration Object (`config.json` or JavaScript API)\nIf you are using a configuration file or passing options directly into a Node.js script via `require('javascript-obfuscator').obfuscate(code, options)`[4], structure your JSON like this[3]:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"compact\": true,\n  \"stringArray\": true,\n  \"stringArrayEncoding\": [\"rc4\"],\n  \"stringArrayThreshold\": 1\n}\n```\n\n### Explanation of these options:\n* **`compact: true`**: Outputs the obfuscated code on a single compressed line without unnecessary whitespace or indentation[5].\n* **`stringArray: true`**: Pulls literal strings out of your source code and puts them into a special array[5].\n* **`stringArrayEncoding: [\"rc4\"]`** *(or `\"rc4\"`)*: Encodes all string array elements using RC4 encryption, making them 